@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Introduction to DevOps";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps"; // Assuming you add DevOps to the main navigation
}

<div class="container-fluid">
    <!-- Changed to container-fluid for full-width layout -->
    <h1 class="display-4">Introduction to DevOps</h1>
    <hr />

    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-md-12">
            <!-- Changed to col-md-12 for full-width content -->
            <!-- What is DevOps? -->
            <h2>What is DevOps?</h2>
            <p>
                DevOps is a <strong>culture</strong>, <strong>set of practices</strong>, and <strong>tools</strong> that aim to improve collaboration between software development (Dev) and IT operations (Ops) teams. The goal is to shorten the development lifecycle, deliver high-quality software faster, and ensure reliable deployments.
            </p>
            <p>
                Think of DevOps as a bridge between two traditionally separate teams:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Development Team:</strong> Writes code and builds new features.</li>
                <li><strong>Operations Team:</strong> Manages servers, deploys software, and ensures systems run smoothly.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                DevOps brings these teams together, enabling them to work as a single unit focused on delivering value to the end user.
            </p>

            <!-- Full-Width Image -->
            <div class="text-center mb-4">
                <img src="~/css/Images/AzureDevOps/DevOps fosters collaboration between Dev and Ops teams.webp"
                     alt="DevOps fosters collaboration between Dev and Ops teams"
                     class="img-fluid rounded"
                     style="width: 60%; height: 300px;">
                <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>DevOps fosters collaboration between Dev and Ops teams.</small></p>
            </div>

            <!-- DevOps vs Traditional IT -->
            <h2>DevOps vs Traditional IT</h2>
            <p>
                In traditional IT, development and operations teams work in silos. Here’s how DevOps differs:
            </p>
            <table class="table table-bordered">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Aspect</th>
                        <th>Traditional IT</th>
                        <th>DevOps</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Team Structure</strong></td>
                        <td>Separate teams for Dev and Ops</td>
                        <td>Collaborative, cross-functional teams</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Communication</strong></td>
                        <td>Limited communication between teams</td>
                        <td>Continuous communication and feedback</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Deployment</strong></td>
                        <td>Manual, infrequent deployments</td>
                        <td>Automated, frequent deployments</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Focus</strong></td>
                        <td>Stability and control</td>
                        <td>Speed, agility, and innovation</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- Benefits of DevOps -->
            <h2>Benefits of DevOps</h2>
            <p>
                Adopting DevOps brings numerous benefits to organizations:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Faster Delivery:</strong> Automation of processes like testing and deployment speeds up software releases. For example, companies like Netflix deploy code thousands of times per day using DevOps practices.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Improved Collaboration:</strong> DevOps breaks down silos between teams, fostering a culture of shared responsibility. Tools like Slack and Microsoft Teams facilitate communication.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Enhanced Quality:</strong> Continuous testing ensures fewer bugs and higher-quality software. Automated testing tools like Selenium and JUnit are commonly used.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Scalability:</strong> DevOps enables organizations to scale infrastructure and applications efficiently. Cloud platforms like AWS and Azure provide scalable solutions.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Reliability:</strong> Automated deployments reduce human errors, making systems more reliable. Tools like Jenkins and GitLab CI/CD ensure consistent deployments.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <!-- DevOps Lifecycle -->
            <h2>DevOps Lifecycle</h2>
            <p>
                The DevOps lifecycle is a continuous process that includes the following stages:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <strong>Plan:</strong> Define the project goals, requirements, and roadmap. Tools like Jira and Trello help in planning and tracking tasks.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Code:</strong> Developers write code using version control systems like Git. Platforms like GitHub and GitLab facilitate collaboration.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Build:</strong> The code is compiled into executable files. Tools like Maven and Gradle automate the build process.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Test:</strong> Automated testing ensures code quality. Tools like Selenium, JUnit, and TestNG are used for testing.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Release:</strong> The application is deployed to a staging environment for final testing. Tools like Jenkins and CircleCI automate the release process.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Deploy:</strong> The application is deployed to production using CI/CD pipelines. Tools like Kubernetes and Docker help in managing deployments.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Operate:</strong> The application is monitored and managed in production. Tools like Nagios and Prometheus provide real-time monitoring.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Monitor:</strong> Continuous monitoring ensures the application runs smoothly. Tools like Splunk and ELK Stack analyze logs and metrics.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <!-- Learning Outcome -->
            <h2>Learning Outcome</h2>
            <p>
                By the end of this module, you will:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Understand the fundamentals of DevOps and its importance in modern software development.</li>
                <li>Learn how DevOps differs from traditional IT practices.</li>
                <li>Explore the benefits of adopting DevOps in an organization.</li>
                <li>Gain insights into the DevOps lifecycle and its key stages.</li>
                <li>Get familiar with popular DevOps tools and technologies.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>